{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 2640 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "8e6768018454714a69b97bebfe38dafe0b09d89a8049bbd3dcf87071fd7ebfb9" :edb/db-before-t 2639 :edb/db-after-t 2640 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535952 50 #inst "2026-10-09T11:25:01.893Z" 13194139535952 true] [17592186147326 1145 "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE $y' = f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nFirst, we need to find the equilibrium solutions. So, we solve $f(y)=0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =0 \\\\ {y}^{3}-2{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2}(y-2) & =0 \\\\ y & =0,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equilibrium solutions are $y=0$ and $y=2.$ \n\nNow, we analyze the sign of $f(y)$ using a sign table, as follows:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty,0)$ | $(0,2)$ | $(2,\\infty)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y^2$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $y-2$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $f(y)$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n\nSo, we have that $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (2,\\infty),$ and negative for $y\\in (-\\infty,0) \\cup (0,2).$\n\nWe can now draw the phase line for this equation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042839/assets/420aaceb6f78b8a6aa55f8e0a2a9a87e604875180a9321ff34bd72ca2384ec09.png)\n\nFinally, we classify the equilibrium solutions:\n\n- $y=0$ is a node because it is neither a source nor a sink.\n\n- $y=2$ is a source because the nearby solutions diverge away from this point.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$y=0$ is a node, and $y=2$ is a source\"." 13194139535952 false] [17592186147326 1145 "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE $y' = f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nFirst, we need to find the equilibrium solutions. So, we solve $f(y)=0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =0 \\\\ {y}^{3}-2{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2}(y-2) & =0 \\\\ y & =0,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equilibrium solutions are $y=0$ and $y=2.$ \n\nNow, we analyze the sign of $f(y)$ using a sign table, as follows:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty,0)$ | $(0,2)$ | $(2,\\infty)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y^2$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $y-2$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $f(y)$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n\nSo, we have that $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (2,\\infty),$ and negative for $y\\in (-\\infty,0) \\cup (0,2).$\n\nWe can now draw the phase line for this equation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/assets/420aaceb6f78b8a6aa55f8e0a2a9a87e604875180a9321ff34bd72ca2384ec09.png)\n\nFinally, we classify the equilibrium solutions:\n\n- $y=0$ is a node because it is neither a source nor a sink.\n\n- $y=2$ is a source because the nearby solutions diverge away from this point.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$y=0$ is a node, and $y=2$ is a source\"." 13194139535952 true] [17592186147327 1100 "A particular autonomous differential equation has the follo