ows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nFirst, we draw the phase line with the given information. \n\nNote that there are $2$ equilibrium points, $y=-2$ and $y=3.$\n\n- We're given that $y=3$ is a sink. We represent this on the phase line by drawing a downward-pointing arrow just above $y=3$ and an upward-pointing arrow just below it.\n\n- We're given that $y=-2$ is a node. This means that the arrows on either side of $y=-2$ point in the same direction. Since the arrow just above $y=-2$ points upward, the arrow just below it points upward too.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/assets/5ea8af3b30df51189c3511dbe093f05b309dc50cbdea1e52d5ba3a42d88101c2.png)\n\nOf the given options, the only slope field that correspond with this phase line is the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/assets/5737491c0a472a12cb3fd9e0b09cb5ede88b193a3f084ca89671e4669b945d5b.png)\n\nNote that:\n\n- The slope field is horizontal at $y=-2$ and $y=3.$\n\n- The slopes near $y=-2$ appear to diverge away from this line when we're looking above the line and appear to converge toward the line when we're looking below the line.\n\n- The slopes near $y=3$ appear to converge toward this line." 13194139535952 true] [17592186376157 1000 #edb/ref 17592186376158 13194139535952 true] [17592186376157 1000 #edb/ref 17592186376159 13194139535952 true] [17592186376157 1000 #edb/ref 17592186376160 13194139535952 true] [17592186376157 1000 #edb/ref 17592186376161 13194139535952 true] [17592186376157 1000 #edb/ref 17592186376162 13194139535952 true] [17592186376157 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535952 true] [17592186376157 1041 "selection" 13194139535952 true] [17592186376157 1104 #edb/ref 17592186376161 13194139535952 true] [17592186376157 1110 #uuid "066a9351-b2a2-5970-8d1f-ebf0bdbaa5a0" 13194139535952 true] [17592186376158 1034 "/home/jake/Devel